{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789854710,"nonce":"ViGv9PsFiUrZFz2hERlYVi2+rj8=","title":"an optional basic-type parameter `?len:Int` is typed Int, so a passed 0 reads as null","message":"Haxe types `?len:Int` (no default) as `Null\u003cInt\u003e`; the typer keeps the annotation, so the parameter lowers as a raw i32 and `len == null` is `len == 0`: `StringBuf.addSub(s, 0, 0)` copies the WHOLE string (`n = sb.length - pos`), which is why `haxe.xml.Parser.parse(\"\u0026lt;\")` yields `\u0026lt;\u003c` -- the escape branch adds the raw text then the entity. `len:Null\u003cInt\u003e` and `?len:Null\u003cInt\u003e` already lower as boxes and behave.\n\nFix: wrap an optional parameter without a default in `Null\u003c\u003e` when its type is Int/Float/Bool, at every site that reads a parameter's type hint (fields.rs lower_parameter/lower_function_param, classes.rs pre-registered signatures, registration.rs, abstracts.rs, expr/mod.rs lambdas). This changes the ABI of every such stdlib method (addSub, Array.slice/splice, substr...), so call-site boxing (`maybe_materialize_for_call`, `fill_default_args`) and the mapping/blade snapshots must follow; TestXML.testCustomXmlParser is the first row it unblocks.\n\nSeparately: `Std.parseInt(\"64\") \u003e= 128` is TRUE -- a relational operator on a `Null\u003cInt\u003e` value compares the box pointer (only `== null` unboxes). `haxe.xml.Parser` throws \"Cannot encode UTF8-char 64\" on `\u0026#64;` because of it.","files":null}]}